  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Saturday...\n\nCooler conditions exist today behind a cold front passage. With \nthis, high temperatures mainly in the 60s are forecast under sunny\nto mostly sunny skies. Winds are also lighter out of the east, \nthough occasional gusts in the teens to low 20s (mph) will occur \nthrough the day. Tonight, low temperatures will generally be in \nthe 30s and 40s (F). Over the plains of eastern Montana (generally\neast of Rosebud County), southeasterly winds will increase \ntonight into Monday, peaking during the day Monday. With this, \nwind gusts in the 20s and 30s (mph) will be common, with a few \nwind gusts into the low 40s (mph) over eastern portions of Fallon \nand Carter Counties. \n\nMonday through mid-week will see a return to seasonably warm \ntemperatures as a ridge of high pressure over the central United \nStates nudges into the region. While a dry cold front passage on \nTuesday may decrease temperatures a bit, highs in the 60s and 70s \nare generally forecast, warmest on Thursday. Through this time, \nmainly dry conditions are forecast, though there is an occasional \nslight chance of precipitation over the mountains of southern \nMontana and northern Wyoming due to a developing low pressure \nsystem off the west coast of the United States. \n\nThis low pressure system is expected to move onshore Thursday and\nprogress northeastward towards our region Friday into Saturday. \nWhile uncertainty remains regarding the evolution of this system \nthrough our region, the most likely scenario at this point looks \nto be a cold front with scattered precipitation as the low \npressure system to the south merges with a trough passing to the \nnorth. How and exactly where these two system merge remains \nuncertain, and this plays a big role in the amount of wind and \nprecipitation we could see. LA",
